<!--
  A phone with a pulse across it: chosen by the product owner from four candidates rendered side by
  side at the sizes an icon is actually met at (screenshots/logo-*.png, since deleted).

  It replaces a 3x3 grid with one dead cell. That mark stated the app's idea precisely and stated it
  to nobody: at 24dp, which is the size in a Play search result, it collapsed into a mush of white
  squares with the red cell all but invisible. Distinctiveness is worth less than legibility for an
  app nobody has heard of.

  ## Geometry, and why these numbers

  Body: 32 x 60 at x 38..70, y 24..84, corner 7.

  Not larger, and this is the constraint that decides the whole composition: a launcher may mask an
  adaptive icon to a CIRCLE of diameter 72, so anything more than 36 from the centre can be cropped.
  A 34x64 body puts its corners at 36.2 and a 40x72 body at 41.2 - both would have had their corners
  shaved off on the phones that use round icons. 32x60 sits at 34.0 and is safe.

  Pulse: stroke 7, inset 5 from each side of the body, spanning x 43..65 about the centre line.

  The inset is the fix for the reviewed version, where the line ran edge to edge and read as cramped
  rather than deliberate. Stroke 7 rather than something finer because 7/108 is about 1.6px at 24dp,
  and anything thinner disappears at the size that matters most.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <path
        android:fillColor="#FAFAFA"
        android:pathData="M45,24h18a7,7 0 0,1 7,7v46a7,7 0 0,1 -7,7h-18a7,7 0 0,1 -7,-7V31a7,7 0 0,1 7,-7z" />

    <path
        android:strokeColor="#22C55E"
        android:strokeWidth="7"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:pathData="M43,54h4l4,-8l5,15l4,-7h5" />
</vector>
